{"task_id": "Mbpp/166", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Iterator\n\ndef find_even_pair(input_list: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of pairs of integers in a list that xor to an even number.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    def _is_even(value: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Determines if a number is even.\"\"\"\n        return value % 2 == 0\n\n    def _calculate_xor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Performs a bitwise XOR operation between two integers.\"\"\"\n        return a ^ b\n    \n    def _generate_pairs(lst: List[int]) -> Iterator[Tuple[int, int]]:\n        \"\"\"Generates all unique pairs of elements from the input list by index.\"\"\"\n        n_lst = len(lst)\n        for i in range(n_lst):\n            for j in range(i + 1, n_lst):\n                yield (lst[i], lst[j])\n\n    even_xor_pairs = 0\n\n    for num1, num2 in _generate_pairs(input_list):\n        xor_result = _calculate_xor(num1, num2)\n        if _is_even(xor_result):\n            even_xor_pairs += 1\n\n    return even_xor_pairs\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/167", "solution": "def next_power_of_2(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest power of 2 greater than or equal to n.\n    Handles n=0 by returning 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # If n is already a power of 2 (e.g., 1, 2, 4), then n itself is the answer.\n    # A positive integer n is a power of 2 if and only if n & (n - 1) is 0.\n    if (n & (n - 1)) == 0:\n        return n\n    else:\n        # If n is not a power of 2, we need the next one.\n        # The exponent for the next power of 2 is simply n.bit_length().\n        # For example, if n=3 (binary 11), bit_length() is 2, next power is 2^2=4.\n        # If n=5 (binary 101), bit_length() is 3, next power is 2^3=8.\n        exponent = n.bit_length()\n        return 1 << exponent"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/170", "solution": "def sum_range_list(numbers, start_index, end_index):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of numbers in a list within the specified (inclusive) index range.\n    Handles out-of-bounds indices and empty lists gracefully by clamping the effective range.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    \n    # Calculate the actual start and end indices for iteration, ensuring they are within valid list bounds.\n    # The iteration will start from 'actual_start' and go up to, but not include, 'actual_end_exclusive'.\n    actual_start = max(0, start_index)\n    actual_end_exclusive = min(len(numbers), end_index + 1)\n\n    for i in range(actual_start, actual_end_exclusive):\n        total_sum += numbers[i]\n        \n    return total_sum"}
