{"task_id": "Mbpp/261", "solution": "from typing import Union\n\ndef _convert_to_int_if_whole(value: float) -> Union[int, float]:\n    \"\"\"Converts a float to an integer if it has no fractional part.\"\"\"\n    if value == int(value):\n        return int(value)\n    return value\n\ndef division_elements(tuple1: tuple, tuple2: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Performs element-wise division on two tuples, returning integer results when exact.\"\"\"\n    results = []\n    for i in range(len(tuple1)):\n        result = tuple1[i] / tuple2[i]\n        results.append(_convert_to_int_if_whole(result))\n    return tuple(results)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/260", "solution": "def newman_prime(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the nth Newman-Shanks-Williams prime number.\n    This is interpreted as the smallest prime S_j such that j >= n,\n    where S_j is the j-th Newman-Shanks-Williams number.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _is_prime(num):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime using trial division.\"\"\"\n        if num < 2:\n            return False\n        if num == 2:\n            return True\n        if num % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n        i = 3\n        while i * i <= num:\n            if num % i == 0:\n                return False\n            i += 2\n        return True\n\n    # The Newman-Shanks-Williams (NSW) numbers are defined by:\n    # S_0 = 1\n    # S_1 = 1\n    # S_k = 2 * S_{k-1} + S_{k-2} for k >= 2\n\n    # Initialize S_{k-2} and S_{k-1} to S_0 and S_1 respectively.\n    s_prev2 = 1  # Corresponds to S_{k-2}\n    s_prev1 = 1  # Corresponds to S_{k-1}\n    current_s = 0 # Placeholder for S_k\n\n    # Determine the starting NSW number S_n\n    if n <= 1:\n        # If n=1, we start checking from S_1.\n        # If n=0 or negative, we assume n=1 as per NFR-002 (positive integer inputs).\n        current_s = 1 # S_1\n    else: # n >= 2\n        # Calculate S_k iteratively up to S_n\n        # The loop runs from k=2 up to k=n.\n        for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n            current_s = 2 * s_prev1 + s_prev2\n            s_prev2 = s_prev1\n            s_prev1 = current_s\n        # After the loop, current_s holds S_n.\n\n    # Now, starting from S_n, find the first prime NSW number.\n    # `current_s` holds S_n (or S_1 if n=1).\n    while True:\n        if _is_prime(current_s):\n            return current_s\n        \n        # If current_s is not prime, calculate the next NSW number.\n        # This will be S_{current_k+1}, where current_k was the index of `current_s`.\n        next_s = 2 * s_prev1 + s_prev2\n        s_prev2 = s_prev1\n        s_prev1 = next_s\n        current_s = next_s"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/264", "solution": "def dog_age(human_age: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates a dog's age in dog years.\n    The first two human years count as 10.5 dog years each, and subsequent years count as 4 dog years.\n    \"\"\"\n    if human_age <= 0:\n        return 0\n    elif human_age == 1:\n        return 10  # 10.5 rounded down to an integer\n    elif human_age == 2:\n        return 21  # 10.5 + 10.5\n    else:\n        return 21 + (human_age - 2) * 4"}
