{"task_id": "Mbpp/285", "solution": "def text_match_two_three(text: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a string contains 'a' followed by exactly two or three 'b' characters.\n    Returns True if a match is found, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(text)\n    for i in range(n):\n        if text[i] == 'a':\n            # Check for 'abb'\n            if i + 3 <= n and text[i+1:i+3] == 'bb':\n                # Ensure it's not 'abbb' or longer 'b' sequence, unless it's 'abbb' itself.\n                # If the next character after 'abb' exists and is 'b', then it's 'abbb' or more.\n                # So we must check 'abbb' separately or ensure the 4th char is NOT 'b'.\n                # A simpler approach is to check for 'abb' AND NOT 'abbb' if we want strictly 2 'b's.\n                # However, the requirement is \"two or three 'b' characters\".\n                # So if 'abb' is found, we need to make sure it's not 'abbb' to avoid double-counting.\n                # Or, we can just check if text[i+1:i+3] == 'bb' and text[i+1:i+4] == 'bbb' as two separate conditions.\n                \n                # Check for 'abb'\n                if i + 3 <= n and text[i+1:i+3] == 'bb':\n                    # If the next char (at i+3) is not 'b' or doesn't exist, it's 'abb'\n                    if i + 3 == n or text[i+3] != 'b':\n                        return True\n                \n                # Check for 'abbb'\n                if i + 4 <= n and text[i+1:i+4] == 'bbb':\n                    # If the next char (at i+4) is not 'b' or doesn't exist, it's 'abbb'\n                    if i + 4 == n or text[i+4] != 'b':\n                        return True\n\n    return False"}
