{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def find_leftmost_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the position (0-indexed) of the leftmost unset bit.\n    This is the highest indexed 0-bit among currently significant bits, or the next available position.\"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    num_bits = n.bit_length()\n    for pos in range(num_bits - 1, -1, -1):\n        if (n >> pos) & 1 == 0:\n            return pos\n    \n    # If all bits up to num_bits - 1 are set (e.g., n = 7 which is 111),\n    # the leftmost unset bit is at position num_bits.\n    return num_bits\n\ndef set_bit_at_position(n: int, pos: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Sets the bit at the specified position to 1.\n    Uses bitwise OR with a mask created by left-shifting 1.\"\"\"\n    return n | (1 << pos)\n\ndef set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Sets the leftmost unset bit in the binary representation of n.\n    It identifies the highest indexed 0-bit and changes it to 1.\"\"\"\n    pos = find_leftmost_unset_bit(n)\n    return set_bit_at_position(n, pos)"}
