{"task_id": "Mbpp/391", "solution": "from typing import List, Any, Dict\n\ndef convert_list_dictionary(list1: List[Any], list2: List[Any], list3: List[Any]) -> List[Dict[Any, Dict[Any, Any]]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert three parallel lists into a list of nested dictionaries.\n\n    Args:\n        list1: Elements become outer dictionary keys\n        list2: Elements become inner dictionary keys\n        list3: Elements become inner dictionary values\n\n    Returns:\n        List of nested dictionaries with structure [{list1[i]: {list2[i]: list3[i]}}]\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If input lists have different lengths\n    \"\"\"\n    if not (len(list1) == len(list2) == len(list3)):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input lists must have the same length.\")\n\n    result_list = []\n    for i in range(len(list1)):\n        nested_dict = {list1[i]: {list2[i]: list3[i]}}\n        result_list.append(nested_dict)\n\n    return result_list"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/392", "solution": "# Memoization cache for storing computed results of f(n).\n_memo = {}\n\ndef get_max_sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum sum for n using the recursive equation f(n) = max((f(n/2) + f(n/3) + f(n/4) + f(n/5)), n).\n    Results are cached to optimize performance for repeated subproblems.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n in _memo:\n        return _memo[n]\n\n    # The equation implicitly handles base cases (e.g., n=0 results in 0, small positive n\n    # will often have max(sum_of_parts, n) evaluate to n as the sum is smaller or equal).\n    # This prevents infinite recursion as n eventually becomes 0 through integer division.\n    \n    sum_of_parts = (\n        get_max_sum(n // 2) +\n        get_max_sum(n // 3) +\n        get_max_sum(n // 4) +\n        get_max_sum(n // 5)\n    )\n\n    result = max(sum_of_parts, n)\n    _memo[n] = result\n    return result"}
