{"task_id": "Mbpp/440", "solution": "import re\n\ndef find_adverb_position(sentence: str) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the first adverb and its positions in a given sentence.\n    Returns a tuple (start_position, end_position, adverb_word).\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Iterate through the sentence to find words and their original positions.\n    # Using re.finditer to capture words (sequences of non-whitespace characters)\n    # and their start indices, effectively implementing `TextProcessor.get_word_positions`.\n    for match in re.finditer(r'\\S+', sentence):\n        original_word_with_punct = match.group(0)\n        start_pos_in_sentence = match.start()\n\n        # Clean punctuation from the word to check if it's an adverb.\n        # This implements `TextProcessor.clean_punctuation`.\n        cleaned_word = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z]', '', original_word_with_punct)\n\n        # Check if the cleaned word is an adverb using a simple heuristic.\n        # This implements `AdverbIdentifier.is_adverb`.\n        # Adverbs often end with 'ly' and should be longer than just 'ly'.\n        if cleaned_word.endswith('ly') and len(cleaned_word) > 2:\n            # If an adverb is found, calculate its end position.\n            # The end position is based on the start position and the length of the cleaned adverb.\n            # This implements `PositionCalculator.calculate_start_position` and `calculate_end_position`.\n            end_pos = start_pos_in_sentence + len(cleaned_word)\n            adverb_word = cleaned_word\n\n            return (start_pos_in_sentence, end_pos, adverb_word)\n\n    # If no adverb is found after checking all words, return a default \"not found\" tuple.\n    return (-1, -1, '')"}
