{"task_id": "Mbpp/603", "solution": "def get_ludic(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates all ludic numbers less than or equal to a given integer n.\n    It uses a sieve method similar to the Sieve of Eratosthenes, but based on positions.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Initialize the list of all natural numbers up to n.\n    # This list will be modified by removing non-ludic numbers.\n    numbers = list(range(1, n + 1))\n    ludic_numbers = []\n\n    # Continue the sieve process as long as there are numbers to consider.\n    while numbers:\n        # The first number in the current 'numbers' list is always the next ludic number.\n        current_ludic = numbers.pop(0)\n        ludic_numbers.append(current_ludic)\n\n        # The number 1 is a special case; it does not sieve other numbers by position.\n        if current_ludic == 1:\n            continue\n\n        new_numbers = []\n        count = 0\n        # Iterate through the remaining numbers to apply the sieve.\n        # Remove every 'current_ludic'-th number based on its current position.\n        for num in numbers:\n            count += 1\n            if count % current_ludic != 0:\n                new_numbers.append(num)\n        numbers = new_numbers\n\n    return ludic_numbers"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/605", "solution": "import math\n\ndef prime_num(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given integer is a prime number using an optimized algorithm.\n    Handles edge cases for 1, 2, and other even numbers efficiently.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    if n == 2:\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n\n    # Check for odd divisors from 3 up to the square root of n\n    limit = int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1\n    for i in range(3, limit, 2):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/603", "solution": "def get_ludic(limit: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates all Ludic numbers less than or equal to the given limit using a sieve-like approach.\n    \"\"\"\n    if limit < 1:\n        return []\n\n    # Initialize a list of candidate numbers from 1 to limit.\n    numbers = list(range(1, limit + 1))\n    ludic_numbers = []\n\n    # The sieve process continues as long as there are candidate numbers.\n    while numbers:\n        # The first number in the remaining list is always a Ludic number.\n        l = numbers.pop(0)\n        ludic_numbers.append(l)\n\n        # If l is 1, it does not sieve any numbers; it's a special starting point.\n        if l == 1:\n            continue\n\n        new_numbers = []\n        count = 0\n        # Iterate through the remaining numbers to sieve.\n        for num in numbers:\n            count += 1\n            # If the current number's position (1-indexed) is a multiple of l, it's removed.\n            if count % l != 0:\n                new_numbers.append(num)\n            # Reset count when a number is removed, effectively restarting the 'l-th' count.\n            else:\n                count = 0\n        numbers = new_numbers\n\n    return ludic_numbers"}
