{"task_id": "Mbpp/739", "solution": "def find_Index(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the index of the smallest triangular number with n digits.\n    Uses a loop to check triangular numbers until the desired digit count is met.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _calculate_triangular_number(k: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the k-th triangular number.\"\"\"\n        return k * (k + 1) // 2\n\n    def _count_digits(number: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Counts the number of digits in an integer.\"\"\"\n        if number == 0:\n            return 1\n        # Convert to string for simple digit counting\n        return len(str(number))\n\n    k = 1\n    while True:\n        triangular_value = _calculate_triangular_number(k)\n        digit_count = _count_digits(triangular_value)\n\n        if digit_count == n:\n            return k\n        \n        # If the number of digits exceeds n, it means we've skipped over the range.\n        # This shouldn't happen if we're looking for the *smallest* n-digit number.\n        # We continue incrementing k until we find a number with exactly n digits.\n        # For instance, if n=2, and we get 1 digit (e.g. T_3=6), we continue.\n        # If we then get 2 digits (e.g. T_4=10), we return 4.\n        # The design implies a direct match for `digit_count == n`.\n        \n        k += 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/739", "solution": "def calculate_triangular_number(k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculate triangular number T(k) = k*(k+1)/2.\"\"\"\n    return (k * (k + 1)) // 2\n\ndef count_digits(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Count number of digits in positive integer.\"\"\"\n    return len(str(number))\n\ndef validate_input(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Validate that n is positive integer.\"\"\"\n    # Per constraints, no isinstance checks or exceptions. Assume n is a positive int.\n    return n > 0\n\ndef find_Index(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Find index of smallest triangular number with n digits.\"\"\"\n    # The design context indicates validate_input should be called.\n    # Given constraints, this is a minimal check assuming valid input type.\n    if not validate_input(n):\n        # In a real scenario, this would raise an error or handle invalid input.\n        # Per constraints, we assume valid input or handle minimally.\n        return -1 # Or some other indicator if 'n' is not positive, though problem context implies n >= 1.\n\n    k = 1\n    while True:\n        triangular_number = calculate_triangular_number(k)\n        num_digits = count_digits(triangular_number)\n\n        if num_digits == n:\n            return k\n        \n        # If the number of digits exceeds n, it means we've overshot.\n        # For triangular numbers, the number of digits is monotonically non-decreasing.\n        # This optimization could be added if performance was critical for very large n,\n        # but for typical cases, simple iteration is fine.\n        # if num_digits > n:\n        #    # This can happen if n is very small and we skip numbers,\n        #    # e.g., if n=1, T(1)=1, T(2)=3, T(3)=6, T(4)=10. If we were looking for 1-digit, k=1, return.\n        #    # If we are looking for n digits, and we reach num_digits > n,\n        #    # it means there's no exact match at this point, but for triangular numbers\n        #    # this implies the previous one was likely the largest with n-1 digits.\n        #    # The current logic will find the *first* exact match.\n\n        k += 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/740", "solution": "```python\ndef tuple_to_dict(input_tuple: tuple) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert tuple to dictionary by pairing adjacent elements.\n    Adjacent elements are used as key-value pairs (element[i]: element[i+1]).\n    \"\"\"\n    keys = input_tuple[0::2]\n    values = input_tuple[1::2]\n    return dict(zip(keys, values))\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/741", "solution": "def all_Characters_Same(input_string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if all characters in the given string are identical.\"\"\"\n    n = len(input_string)\n\n    # Empty string or single-character string are considered to have all same characters.\n    if n <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    # Compare all characters to the first character.\n    first_char = input_string[0]\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if input_string[i] != first_char:\n            return False\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/740", "solution": "def tuple_to_dict(input_tuple: tuple) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Converts a tuple to a dictionary using adjacent elements as key-value pairs.\n    The last element is ignored if the tuple has an odd number of elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_dict = {}\n    # Iterate through the tuple, stepping by 2 to get adjacent pairs.\n    # The loop stops before the last element if the tuple length is odd,\n    # effectively ignoring the unpaired element.\n    for i in range(0, len(input_tuple) - 1, 2):\n        key = input_tuple[i]\n        value = input_tuple[i + 1]\n        result_dict[key] = value\n    return result_dict"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/741", "solution": "def all_Characters_Same(input_string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Check whether all characters in a string are the same.\n\n    Args:\n        input_string (str): The string to validate\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if all characters are identical, False otherwise\n    \"\"\"\n    if not input_string:\n        return True\n\n    first_char = input_string[0]\n    for char in input_string[1:]:\n        if char != first_char:\n            return False\n\n    return True"}
