{"task_id": "Mbpp/803", "solution": "def is_perfect_square(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given integer is a perfect square.\n    Returns True if n is a perfect square, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        return False\n    \n    # Calculate the integer square root.\n    # Using n**0.5 leverages efficient underlying square root computation.\n    # int() truncates the float result to its integer part.\n    root = int(n**0.5)\n    \n    # A number is a perfect square if the square of its integer square root\n    # is equal to the original number.\n    return root * root == n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/804", "solution": "def is_product_even(numbers: list[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the product of numbers in a list is even.\n    The product is considered even if the list contains zero or any even number.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        # As per design context (empty list use case sequence diagram),\n        # return True for an empty list. The empty product is 1, which is odd,\n        # but the specific design sequence diagram dictates True.\n        return True\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        if num == 0:\n            # If any number is 0, the product is 0, which is even.\n            return True\n        if num % 2 == 0:\n            # If any number is even, the entire product will be even.\n            return True\n    \n    # If no even numbers or zero were found, all numbers are odd.\n    # The product of only odd numbers is always odd.\n    return False"}
