{"task_id": "Mbpp/804", "solution": "from typing import List, Union\n\nNumber = Union[int, float]\n\ndef is_product_even(numbers: List[Number]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the product of numbers in a list is even.\n    Returns True if the product is even, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return False\n\n    # Optimization: if any number is 0, the product is 0, which is even.\n    if 0 in numbers:\n        return True\n\n    product = 1\n    for num in numbers:\n        # Optimization: if any number is even, the product is even.\n        # This handles both int and float (where float can be even like 2.0).\n        # For floats, we only care if they are 'effectively' even or odd when multiplied,\n        # but for this problem, if an integer factor is even, the product is even.\n        # If we encounter a float, we must multiply it fully.\n        if isinstance(num, int) and num % 2 == 0:\n            return True # If an integer factor is even, the final product will be even.\n        product *= num\n\n    # After iterating, if no integer factor was even,\n    # check the final product's evenness.\n    # Note: For floats, `product % 2 == 0` correctly identifies if the float product\n    # is an even number (e.g., 4.0 % 2 == 0 is True, 3.0 % 2 == 0 is False).\n    return product % 2 == 0"}
